IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ucta-nouns`
IMPORT `ucta-logic`

-- Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation.
-- LAYER 1, MODULE 7: PART 1, AVOIDANCE OF LIABILITY FOR NEGLIGENCE, BREACH OF CONTRACT, ETC., sections 2 to 4
-- (Negligence liability; Liability arising in contract; Unreasonable indemnity clauses).
--
-- Each function takes `Reach of a section`: the answer of `s 1(2) and (3) whether an exception takes the
-- section out of the case` (module ucta-p1-introductory).
--   * Where a flat negative takes the section out of the case (First Schedule "do not extend", s 1(3) "only",
--     a Part 3 provision), the "requirement of reasonableness applies" twin answers JUST FALSE (the text's own no).
--     A function that asks whether the person CAN exclude or rely answers NOTHING there: the Act then says
--     nothing about whether they can (a lifted bar grants nothing).
--   * Where the reach "cannot be told", every function answers NOTHING.
--
-- Where a subsection says "cannot ... except in so far as the term satisfies the requirement of
-- reasonableness" (ruling QA, 2026-11-04, by the text's own spelling): JUST FALSE if the term is not shown
-- to be reasonable; NOTHING if it is shown reasonable (the bar is lifted and nothing is granted).
-- Section 11(5) puts the burden of showing reasonableness on the party who claims it, so `reasonable` is
-- read as "it is shown that the term satisfies the requirement": JUST TRUE yes; JUST FALSE shown not;
-- NOTHING nobody has shown it, which is treated like "not shown" (fork F13 in NOTES.md).
-- Each such site has a "requirement of reasonableness applies" twin that answers JUST TRUE in the zone.
--
-- READING A RESULT (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").
-- A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.

§ `Section 2 - negligence liability`

-- src:117 | 2.—(1) A person cannot by reference to any contract term or to a
-- src:118 | notice given to persons generally or to particular persons exclude or
-- src:119 | restrict his liability for death or personal injury resulting from
-- src:120 | negligence.
@ref s 2(1) (src:117-120)
GIVEN loss IS A `Kind of loss`
      `the liability results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) the person can exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (reach EQUALS `No exception takes the section out of the case`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 1(2) and (3) and the First Schedule take the section out of the case, or whether they do cannot be told
        THEN NOTHING
        IF loss EQUALS `Other loss or damage`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) speaks of "death or personal injury"; other loss or damage is subsection (2)
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT `the liability results from negligence`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) speaks of liability "resulting from negligence" (s 1(1) defines it)
        THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person cannot by reference to any contract term or to a notice given to persons generally or to particular persons exclude or restrict his liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:122 | (2) In the case of other loss or damage, a person cannot so exclude
-- src:123 | or restrict his liability for negligence except in so far as the term or
-- src:124 | notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
@ref s 2(2), the requirement applies (src:122-124)
GIVEN loss IS A `Kind of loss`
      `the liability results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the term or notice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF reach EQUALS `An exception takes the section out of the case`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): a flat negative takes the section out of the case -- section 1(3), sections 2 to 7 apply "only to business liability"; the First Schedule, "do not extend to"; or a Part 3 provision ("do not apply", "are not subject to", "do not operate", "is not precluded", "Nothing in this Act applies") -- so no requirement of reasonableness applies under this section
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF NOT (reach EQUALS `No exception takes the section out of the case`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule or Part 3 take the section out of the case cannot be told (whether the person protected deals as consumer is not known and the answers differ, or an input outside the text decides it)
        THEN NOTHING
        IF loss EQUALS `Death or personal injury`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) is "In the case of other loss or damage"; death or personal injury is subsection (1), which has no reasonableness exception
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT `the liability results from negligence`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) speaks of liability "for negligence"
        THEN NOTHING
        -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "except in so far as the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:122 | (2) In the case of other loss or damage, a person cannot so exclude
-- src:123 | or restrict his liability for negligence except in so far as the term or
-- src:124 | notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
@ref s 2(2) (src:122-124)
GIVEN loss IS A `Kind of loss`
      `the liability results from negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      reasonable IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) the person can exclude or restrict liability for other loss or damage resulting from negligence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`s 2(2) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the term or notice` loss `the liability results from negligence` reach EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND (NOT (reasonable EQUALS JUST TRUE))
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person cannot so exclude or restrict his liability for negligence except in so far as the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness" -- the term or notice is not shown to satisfy it: it is for those claiming that it does to show that it does (s 11(5)), so a term not shown reasonable (unreasonable, or reasonableness not shown) is the bar's case
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the term or notice is shown to be reasonable, so the bar is lifted "in so far as" and the subsection grants nothing (ruling QA, by the text's spelling "cannot ... except"); or the subsection does not reach the case
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:126 | (3) Where a contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict
-- src:127 | liability for negligence, a person’s agreement to or awareness of it is
-- src:128 | not of itself to be taken as indicating his voluntary acceptance of any
-- src:129 | risk.
@ref s 2(3) (src:126-129)
GIVEN `the contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) the person's agreement to or awareness of the term or notice is of itself to be taken as indicating voluntary acceptance of the risk` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (reach EQUALS `No exception takes the section out of the case`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 1(2) and (3) and the First Schedule take the section out of the case, or whether they do cannot be told
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT `the contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where a contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence" -- the subsection does not say that agreement or awareness IS acceptance of the risk; the question in the subsection's own words is `s 2(3) the person's agreement to or awareness of the term or notice is not of itself to be taken as indicating voluntary acceptance of the risk`, which answers JUST FALSE outside the "Where" (fork F6, site s 2(3))
        THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person's agreement to or awareness of it is not of itself to be taken as indicating his voluntary acceptance of any risk"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:126 | (3) Where a contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict
-- src:127 | liability for negligence, a person’s agreement to or awareness of it is
-- src:128 | not of itself to be taken as indicating his voluntary acceptance of any
-- src:129 | risk.
@ref s 2(3), the provision applies (src:126-129)
GIVEN `the contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) the person's agreement to or awareness of the term or notice is not of itself to be taken as indicating voluntary acceptance of the risk` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF reach EQUALS `An exception takes the section out of the case`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): a flat negative takes the section out of the case -- section 1(3), sections 2 to 7 apply "only to business liability"; the First Schedule, "do not extend to"; or a Part 3 provision -- so the subsection does not apply
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF NOT (reach EQUALS `No exception takes the section out of the case`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule or Part 3 take the section out of the case cannot be told
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT `the contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words; marginal reading for a lawyer, a failed "Where" on a negative statement is the text's no, ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)): "Where a contract term or notice purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence, a person's agreement to or awareness of it is not of itself to be taken as indicating his voluntary acceptance of any risk" -- nothing purports to exclude or restrict liability for negligence, so the subsection does not apply
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "a person's agreement to or awareness of it is not of itself to be taken as indicating his voluntary acceptance of any risk"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 3 - liability arising in contract`

-- src:133 | 3.—(1) This section applies as between contracting parties where
-- src:134 | one of them deals as consumer or on the other’s written standard
-- src:135 | terms of business.
@ref s 3(1) (src:133-135)
GIVEN `the person relying on the term and the other person are contracting parties` IS A BOOLEAN
      `one of them deals as consumer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `one of them deals on the other's written standard terms of business` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) this section applies as between the contracting parties` MEANS
    CONSIDER (`known to be` `the person relying on the term and the other person are contracting parties`) `and also` (`one of them deals as consumer` `or else` (`known to be` `one of them deals on the other's written standard terms of business`))
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "This section applies as between contracting parties where one of them deals as consumer or on the other's written standard terms of business"
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words; marginal reading for a lawyer, a failed "where" on a positive statement of application is the text's no): the "where" is not met
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether one of them deals as consumer (section 12) is not known and the other limbs do not settle it
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:137 | (2) As against that party, the other cannot by reference to any
-- src:138 | contract term —
-- src:140 | (a) when himself in breach of contract, exclude or restrict any
-- src:141 | liability of his in respect of the breach; or
-- src:143 | 31.12.2021
-- src:144 | 4 Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 2020 Ed.
-- src:146 | (b) claim to be entitled —
-- src:148 | (i) to render a contractual performance substantially
-- src:149 | different from that which was reasonably expected of
-- src:150 | him; or
-- src:152 | (ii) in respect of the whole or any part of his contractual
-- src:153 | obligation, to render no performance at all,
-- src:155 | except in so far as (in any of the cases mentioned in this subsection)
-- src:156 | the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
@ref s 3(2), the requirement applies (src:137-156)
GIVEN `section 3 applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      relied IS A `What is relied on`
      claim IS A `What a term claims`
      `the person relying on the term is himself in breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(2) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF reach EQUALS `An exception takes the section out of the case`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): a flat negative takes the section out of the case -- section 1(3), sections 2 to 7 apply "only to business liability"; the First Schedule, "do not extend to"; or a Part 3 provision ("do not apply", "are not subject to", "do not operate", "is not precluded", "Nothing in this Act applies") -- so no requirement of reasonableness applies under this section
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF NOT (`section 3 applies` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) operates "As against that party", the party subsection (1) describes
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (reach EQUALS `No exception takes the section out of the case`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule or Part 3 take the section out of the case cannot be told (whether the person protected deals as consumer is not known and the answers differ, or an input outside the text decides it)
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (relied EQUALS `A contract term`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "by reference to any contract term"; a notice is not named (a notice having contractual effect is neither a contract term nor a notice not having contractual effect, a fork in NOTES.md)
        THEN NOTHING
        IF claim EQUALS `Something else`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) names three cases, (a) exclusion of liability for the person's own breach, (b)(i) substantially different performance, (b)(ii) no performance
        THEN NOTHING
        IF (claim EQUALS `Excludes or restricts the other party's liability for its own breach of contract`) AND (NOT `the person relying on the term is himself in breach of contract`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): (a) speaks of the person "when himself in breach of contract"
        THEN NOTHING
        -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "except in so far as (in any of the cases mentioned in this subsection) the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:137 | (2) As against that party, the other cannot by reference to any
-- src:138 | contract term —
-- src:140 | (a) when himself in breach of contract, exclude or restrict any
-- src:141 | liability of his in respect of the breach; or
-- src:143 | 31.12.2021
-- src:144 | 4 Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 2020 Ed.
-- src:146 | (b) claim to be entitled —
-- src:148 | (i) to render a contractual performance substantially
-- src:149 | different from that which was reasonably expected of
-- src:150 | him; or
-- src:152 | (ii) in respect of the whole or any part of his contractual
-- src:153 | obligation, to render no performance at all,
-- src:155 | except in so far as (in any of the cases mentioned in this subsection)
-- src:156 | the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
@ref s 3(2) (src:137-156)
GIVEN `section 3 applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      relied IS A `What is relied on`
      claim IS A `What a term claims`
      `the person relying on the term is himself in breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      reasonable IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(2) the other party can rely on the contract term as against that party` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`s 3(2) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` `section 3 applies` relied claim `the person relying on the term is himself in breach of contract` reach EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND (NOT (reasonable EQUALS JUST TRUE))
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the other cannot by reference to any contract term (a) when himself in breach of contract, exclude or restrict any liability of his in respect of the breach; or (b) claim to be entitled (i) to render a contractual performance substantially different ...; or (ii) ... to render no performance at all, except in so far as ... the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness" -- the term is not shown to satisfy it (it is for those claiming that it does to show that it does, s 11(5))
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the term is shown to be reasonable, so the bar is lifted "in so far as" and the subsection grants nothing (ruling QA, by the text's spelling "cannot ... except"); or the subsection does not reach the case
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `Section 4 - unreasonable indemnity clauses`

-- src:160 | 4.—(1) A person dealing as consumer cannot by reference to any
-- src:161 | contract term be made to indemnify another person (whether a party
-- src:162 | to the contract or not) in respect of liability that may be incurred by
-- src:163 | the other for negligence or breach of contract, except in so far as the
-- src:164 | contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
@ref s 4(1), the requirement applies (src:160-164)
GIVEN `the person deals as consumer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the term makes the person indemnify another person in respect of liability that may be incurred by the other for negligence or breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      relied IS A `What is relied on`
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF reach EQUALS `An exception takes the section out of the case`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): a flat negative takes the section out of the case -- section 1(3), sections 2 to 7 apply "only to business liability"; the First Schedule, "do not extend to"; or a Part 3 provision ("do not apply", "are not subject to", "do not operate", "is not precluded", "Nothing in this Act applies") -- so no requirement of reasonableness applies under this section
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF NOT (`the person deals as consumer` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A person dealing as consumer cannot ..."; section 12 says whether the person deals as consumer, and if it is not shown the subsection says nothing
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT `the term makes the person indemnify another person in respect of liability that may be incurred by the other for negligence or breach of contract`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "be made to indemnify another person ... in respect of liability that may be incurred by the other for negligence or breach of contract"
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (reach EQUALS `No exception takes the section out of the case`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): whether section 1(2) and (3), the First Schedule or Part 3 take the section out of the case cannot be told (whether the person protected deals as consumer is not known and the answers differ, or an input outside the text decides it)
        THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (relied EQUALS `A contract term`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "by reference to any contract term"; a notice is not named (a notice having contractual effect is a fork in NOTES.md)
        THEN NOTHING
        -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "except in so far as the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:160 | 4.—(1) A person dealing as consumer cannot by reference to any
-- src:161 | contract term be made to indemnify another person (whether a party
-- src:162 | to the contract or not) in respect of liability that may be incurred by
-- src:163 | the other for negligence or breach of contract, except in so far as the
-- src:164 | contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.
@ref s 4(1) (src:160-164)
GIVEN `the person deals as consumer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the term makes the person indemnify another person in respect of liability that may be incurred by the other for negligence or breach of contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      relied IS A `What is relied on`
      reasonable IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      reach IS A `Reach of a section`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(1) the person dealing as consumer can be made to indemnify the other person` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`s 4(1) a requirement of reasonableness applies to the contract term` `the person deals as consumer` `the term makes the person indemnify another person in respect of liability that may be incurred by the other for negligence or breach of contract` relied reach EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND (NOT (reasonable EQUALS JUST TRUE))
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person dealing as consumer cannot by reference to any contract term be made to indemnify another person ... except in so far as the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness" -- the term is not shown to satisfy it (it is for those claiming that it does to show that it does, s 11(5))
        THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the term is shown to be reasonable, so the bar is lifted "in so far as" and the subsection grants nothing (ruling QA, by the text's spelling "cannot ... except"); or the subsection does not reach the case
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:166 | (2) This section applies whether the liability in question —
-- src:168 | (a) is directly that of the person to be indemnified or is
-- src:169 | incurred by him vicariously;
-- src:171 | (b) is to the person dealing as consumer or to someone else.
@ref s 4(2) (src:166-171)
GIVEN how IS A `How the liability is incurred`
      whom IS A `To whom the liability is owed`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4(2) section 4 applies to the liability` MEANS
    CONSIDER how
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "This section applies whether the liability in question (a) is directly that of the person to be indemnified or is incurred by him vicariously"; (b) "is to the person dealing as consumer or to someone else" -- both values of whom are named, so it does not change the answer
    WHEN `Directly by the person to be indemnified` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `Vicariously by the person to be indemnified` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): (a) names two ways, direct and vicarious; any other way is not mentioned
    WHEN `In another way` THEN NOTHING
